WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.000
How many alien races are there, anyway?

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.819
Welcome to the Science Asylum.

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:08.900
I am Nick Lucid.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.960
We noticed something weird happening around
KIC 8462852.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.420
That’s a star, in case you’re wondering.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.340
There a massive structure of small irregularly-shaped
objects.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.080
Aliens?

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.380
Probably not aliens.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.060
It could be a whole bunch of comets, but they’re
not moving like comets usually do.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.100
Definitely aliens.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.840
Alright, alright, let’s consider aliens
for a few minutes.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.230
It would help to know how many aliens 
there might be

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.240
and it shouldn’t be surprising that people
have had this discussion before.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.000
Arguably, the most famous estimates come from
the Drake Equation.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.269
It considers a bunch of factors we care about:
The rate at which stars are born in our galaxy,

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:45.289
The fraction of stars that have planets,
The average number of planets per star that

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:48.510
could support life,
The fraction of those planets that actually

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.539
develop life,
and the fraction of those planets that develop

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:52.819
intelligent life.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.579
It also considers some other factors we don’t
care about like:

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:59.640
The fraction of intelligent races that learn
to communicate by radio wave,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.659
and the length of time those races use those
radio waves.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.880
This equation is a great place to start because
it’s just a bunch of multiplying.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.940
Unfortunately, we don’t care if we can communicate
with them... just if they exist.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.740
I mean, they’re probably not screaming in
all directions via radio waves, anyway,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.780
so let’s tweak this a little.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.890
First, we can get rid of all that radio stuff,
but now the units are in civilizations per

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.410
year, which isn’t very helpful.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.050
We could include how long the civilizations
last before going extinct,

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.030
but that’s kind of depressing,
so let’s just imagine they evolve beyond

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.240
the universe or something.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.420
Multiplying by a time gives us units of civilizations,
solving our problem.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.000
It still doesn’t consider space travel and
the colonization of other planets,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.460
but that calculation is way more complicated.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.760
We’ve got pretty good estimates for the
first few numbers.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.060
Both NASA and the European Space Agency say
the rate of star formation is:

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.300
7 stars per year.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.360
Thanks mostly to Kepler, we know what fraction
of stars have planets:

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.200
Pretty much all of them,
so the fraction is about 1 or 100%.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.700
The average number of planets per star that
could support life is a bit smaller.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.520
For a planet or large moon to habitable, it
has to be a certain distance from its star.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.980
Not too hot, not too cold, but just right,
which is why we call it

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.340
the “Goldilocks Zone.”

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.360
Also, thanks to Kepler, this turns out to
be about 0.4 planets per star.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.290
In other words, about 40% of stars have one
planet in their Goldilocks zone.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.780
The rest of the numbers we know almost nothing
about,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.560
which means we get to talk out of our butts.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.840
Oh, oh, can I start?

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.440
Sure.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:22.240
Yeah!

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.660
If life can develop, it will develop.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.780
Life is awesome like that.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.200
Intelligent life is the ultimate goal of life.

00:02:28.209 --> 00:02:29.209
Because, duh.

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:31.379
And civilizations? They last forever.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.620
You can’t use infinity.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:33.480
Aww man!

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.540
OK, let’s go with a billion years.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.780
OK, so if we take that really optimistic viewpoint seriously,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.320
When we multiply everything out, we get about 2.8 billion civilizations in our galaxy.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.859
Since there are about 100 billion stars in
the galaxy

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.540
that’s 1 civilization for every 35 stars,
which is pretty impressive.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.840
Yeah, it is!

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.120
Ahhh!!!

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:55.960
Don’t be ridiculous!

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.200
Bad clone! Bad!

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:57.980
Sorry.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.100
Whether or not life will develop depends on
so many factors.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:01.980
So many.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.020
The ultimate goal of life is just to survive
and reproduce.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.340
There’s no reason to think intelligent life
is anything special.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.980
Also, we’re on the verge of destroying ourselves,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.840
so I’m surprised we’ve lasted this long.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.160
That's awfully pessimistic, but let's take a look anyway.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.660
Multiplying that out, we get about 1 times 10
to the negative 11 civilizations

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.420
or 1 civilization for every 100 billion galaxies.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.760
Since there are only about 100 billion galaxies
in the universe,

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.080
that’s one intelligent civilization for
the whole universe.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.880
Us.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.660
That’s way too pessimistic and we all know
there's at least two.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.000
[Alien Noises]

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.280
I’m personally a bit more moderate in my
estimates.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:42.470
I get a little over 400 civilizations in our galaxy,
which means the civilizations are an average

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.640
of about 5,000 light years apart.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.600
Considering KIC 8462852 is about 1500 light
years from Earth

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.280
and that civilizations probably won’t be
evenly spaced.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.120
It’s not out of the question to think it
could be an alien structure around that star.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.889
So, are you an optimist, a pessimist, or a
moderate?

00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:00.150
Let us know in the comments.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.920
Thanks for liking, sharing, and subscribing.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.420
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

